.TH SURVEILLANCE 1 "2026-11-01" "0.16.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
surveillance \- Synology Surveillance Station client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B surveillance
.RB [ \-\-debug ]
.RB [ \-\-log\-file [\fB=\fPPATH]]
.RB [ \-\-grid
.IR LAYOUT ]
.RB [ \-\-camera
.I ID
.RB [ \-\-solo ]]
.RB [ \-\-seek
.IR TIME " | " \-\-live ]
.RB [ \-\-page
.IR PAGE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B surveillance
is a native GTK4 desktop client for Synology Surveillance Station.
It connects to a Synology NAS via its REST API to provide live camera views,
recording playback, PTZ control, snapshots, events/alerts, and home mode management.
.SH FEATURES
.TP
.B Live View
View live camera streams in configurable grid layouts (1x1, 2x2, 3x3, 4x4),
selected from the grid button in the header bar.
Each layout remembers its own camera arrangement independently.
A single slot is emptied from the camera sidebar, the whole layout from the
grid menu after a confirmation prompt.
Scroll the mouse wheel over a slot to zoom in, centered on the cursor;
click and drag to pan.
On a dewarped fisheye camera they steer the view instead, as below.
Zoom resets when switching layouts or leaving the page.
A fisheye camera can be shown dewarped, chosen with
.B Fisheye dewarp
in its Camera Settings, with
.B Fisheye mount
for how it hangs.
On a ceiling, looking straight down,
.I Panorama
unrolls the circle into two stacked 180 degree strips with the rim on top,
turned by dragging sideways; on a wall, looking straight out, it is one
strip as wide as the lens sees with the horizon across the middle, which
does not move.
The mouse wheel does nothing on a panorama.
.I Virtual PTZ
shows a flat view steered by dragging and zoomed with the mouse wheel,
turning all the way round on a ceiling and as far as the lens sees on a
wall, but never past straight up or down.
Both apply to live and recorded video in Live View, where they keep their
view across a Reload and reset like the zoom, and to the playback window
recordings open in.
A graphics driver that cannot run the dewarp shows the plain picture.
A lens that is not a centred full circle of 180 degrees is fitted with
.B Calibrate\[u2026]
next to
.BR "Fisheye lens" ,
which draws the lens circle over the camera's current picture; the result
is saved with the camera's other settings on
.BR Apply .
Streams are muted by default; hovering a slot reveals a toolbar with
mute/volume (remembered per camera) and a Snapshot button.
Audio reaches the player over the RTSP-family protocols
.RB ( rtsp ,
.BR rtsp_over_http ,
.BR multicast ,
.BR direct ),
or over the default
.B auto/websocket
protocol when the camera's audio codec is PCMU or AAC (muxed in via
ffmpeg); otherwise the mute button stays greyed out until the protocol is
changed (or DSM reports a different codec) by right-clicking the camera
in the sidebar. A camera with no audio track gets no mute button at all.
A muxed camera that stops sending audio for three seconds while its video
keeps arriving keeps playing without sound until its stream is restarted,
rather than holding up its own video. Its volume control greys out as soon
as that happens. In History mode a gap is filled with silence instead,
since DSM sends no audio at some playback speeds or in reverse. Sound
returns automatically when DSM resumes sending audio at supported playback
speeds.
Cameras with a speaker also show a push-to-talk microphone button \(em tap to
start talking, tap again to stop.
Video is rendered via mpv with hardware acceleration.
While a slot plays recorded video from close to the live edge, the client
also re-asks DSM every few seconds how far that recording now runs, since
one still being written grows after it was first looked up.
The client sends a periodic keepalive to hold each WebSocket session open;
if a session is ever interrupted anyway, the slot reconnects on the same
pipe transparently, with no visible interruption. A camera reported
disabled or disconnected shows
an
.RI ( offline )
placeholder instead of freezing, and a stream that stops responding
mid-session shows
.RI ( "stream lost" )
or
.RI ( "attempting reconnect" )
while retrying. Both recover automatically once the camera is reachable
again, and a slot that was showing recorded video comes back to the same
moment rather than jumping to live.
A slot keeps its stream when a layout switch leaves its camera in the
same position, so a single feed that has drifted behind or stopped
moving is restarted from
.B Reload
in the slot's right-click menu, without disturbing the other slots; a
slot showing recorded video reloads at the same moment rather than
returning to live. The reload button in the header bar does the same for
every slot in the layout, one after another rather than all at once.
Receipts from Surveillance Station Transactions appear over the camera
each POS device is paired with: every new receipt is drawn at the
top-left of every live slot showing that camera and disappears after a
set time. While no paired camera is on screen, only the pairing is read,
every five minutes, and the overlay switches itself off when the NAS has
no Transactions add-on or the account lacks the Transactions privilege.
.TP
.B Live View Timeline
A shared timeline strip below the grid, toggled from the header bar
(persists across restarts). Scroll or use the zoom buttons to zoom the
time scale (centered on the cursor), click-and-drag to pan; a thin blue
border marks which slot's camera the timeline is currently tracking
\(em it follows the last-clicked slot, defaults to the upper-left slot
on layout load, and fades after 10 seconds of no activity anywhere in
the window. Hovering the ruler shows a small preview thumbnail of that
camera at the hovered time, with its date/time overlaid. In History
mode, a date/time bubble follows the marker as it moves. Below the
ruler, a recording-presence bar shows two rows: where the tracked
slot's own camera has a recording, and where any camera in the current
layout does.
.IP
Clicking a point on the ruler switches every slot in the current
layout into History mode, each playing its own camera's recorded video
from that time; a
.B Live
button returns every slot to the real-time stream. Picking a different
camera into a slot already in History mode keeps playing recorded
video, for the newly picked camera, at the same point in time, rather
than dropping back to live; switching grid layout always returns to
live first. Pan/tilt/zoom, focus, preset, patrol, and push-to-talk are
unavailable while a slot shows recorded video (mute/volume and
Snapshot remain available). Jump \(+-10s buttons seek every slot in
the current layout the same way clicking the ruler does. A
.B Pause
button freezes every active slot in place \(em a live slot just
freezes locally without leaving Live mode, while a History slot asks
DSM to actually stop sending, so the gap behind wall clock grows for
as long as it stays paused; resuming a History slot paused for under
10 seconds lands at least 10 seconds behind live rather than right at
the edge. A playback-speed dropdown (History mode only) offers 1/8x
through 100x plus a Fwd/Rev direction toggle, applied to every active
History slot at once; it resets to 1x/Fwd whenever a slot returns to
Live or the layout switches. The higher speeds are greyed out on the
larger layouts, since DSM really does send that many more frames per
second and every slot has to decode them: by default, 1x1 offers the
full range, 2x2 stops at 16x, 3x3 at 8x, and 4x4 at 4x (adjustable from
the Settings page; 1x is always offered, whatever the budget). Real motion/alarm events are overlaid on
the same two presence rows as orange markers.
.B Previous event
and
.B Next event
jump to the nearest one on either side of the current position, across
every camera in the layout; Previous stays available in Live mode
(dropping into History first, like Back 10s), while Next is
History-only, like Forward 10s. Shortly after landing on an event,
Previous goes on to the one before it; once it has been watched for a
while, Previous returns to its start instead. How long counts as
shortly grows with how wide the timeline is zoomed (10 to 30 seconds)
and with the playback speed. Next skips only what a jump has just
landed on. Playing in reverse it is all mirrored: events are entered at
their end, and the grace period applies to Next. Events already loaded
for the timeline are used straight away; past those, the search widens
step by step (an hour, a day, a week) while the button pulses. Events
within 10 seconds of wall clock are skipped, since a seek that close
would just be clamped back to the live edge anyway. A calendar button opens a date/time picker, its own
days marked and any day without a recording (across the current
layout) refused, and its
.B Jump
button greyed out until the exact date/time selected falls within a
real recording; jumping there re-centers the timeline at its default
zoom, while Back/Forward 10s and Previous/Next event instead just pan
(at whatever zoom is already set) if their own target would otherwise
land off-screen. A
.B Filter events
button narrows the presence bar's event markers and Previous/Next
event navigation down to chosen event types. On Surveillance Station 9.3
and later it lists every event type the NAS supports and matches any of
the selected ones. Before 9.3 it matches any or all of them, decoded per
camera brand, and opening it scans each layout camera's full recording
history for the types it has ever produced (a persisted,
incrementally-updated cache, so only the first scan and any time elapsed
since the last one cost real seconds), showing a per-camera progress
checklist meanwhile. While a filter is active, the button's icon is
drawn in the same orange as the event markers. A
.B Download
button opens a popup with a camera picker (only cameras currently
assigned to a slot in the layout, since some may be empty, and
defaulting to the tracked slot's own camera), above two tabs: Quick
Save, whose three one-click buttons immediately prompt for a save
location and download around the tracked slot's current position \(em
.B "Download last 1/2/5 min"
up to it in Live mode, or
.B "Download \-1/2/5 to +1/2/5 min"
centered on it in History mode, since both directions are already
available once paused on a moment of interest \(em and Custom Save,
with Start/End date-time fields (defaulting to a short clip ending at
the same point) for an exact range. Either tab can pick a range that
starts before or runs past what has actually been recorded, easy to do
close to live; the clip is trimmed to what exists rather than refused.
A range with no recording in it at all is refused rather than filled
from the nearest recording.
.TP
.B Camera Sidebar
Lists the cameras alphabetically and switches between pages.
The panel button in the header bar hides or shows it, and that choice is
remembered across restarts.
.TP
.B Recording Playback
Browse, play back, and download recordings filtered by camera and date.
Quick date presets (Today, Yesterday, Last 24 hrs, Last 7 days) for one-click filtering,
plus advanced search by camera(s) and custom time range. Reset button clears all filters.
Active filter summary always visible. Per-event thumbnails are shown for each recording.
The playback window supports scroll-to-zoom (centered on the cursor) and
click-and-drag panning, same as Live View, and dewarps a fisheye camera
the way Live View does.
.TP
.B Camera Controls
Control pan-tilt-zoom cameras with Pan/Tilt, Zoom, Focus, Preset, and Patrol
controls, in the same per-slot hover toolbar as Live View's audio controls.
Picking a patrol asks Surveillance Station to run that saved route; the NAS
drives the camera, so it keeps going after you switch cameras or quit.
Routes are created and edited in Surveillance Station itself, and there is
no stop control, the same as Synology's own clients.
.TP
.B Snapshots
Browse saved snapshots, filter by camera and time range, view, download,
and delete. A camera filter searches at most 5,000 snapshots of the time
range, and the page count says so when the range holds more; narrow the
range to reach the rest. A snapshot can also be taken from a Live View slot's right-click
menu, which saves it to the snapshot database and offers a local copy.
The full-size viewer supports scroll-to-zoom (centered on the cursor) and
click-and-drag panning.
.TP
.B Time Lapse
Browse, play back, download, and manage Smart Time Lapse recordings.
.TP
.B Maps
Show the E-Maps set up in Surveillance Station, with a dot for every camera
placed on a map: green while the camera is enabled, red when it is
disconnected or disabled, grey for one the account cannot see. Hovering a dot
names the camera, and clicking it puts the camera in Live View the way a click
in the camera sidebar does. PNG, JPEG and SVG map images are drawn.
.IP
Maps are created and edited here too: the + button makes a new map from an
image file (the NAS stores PNG; JPEG and SVG are converted), the pencil
switches the selected map to editing, where dots are dragged into place,
cameras added from the sidebar's list and removed with a right click, and
Save sends the whole marker list after asking (Surveillance Station replaces
the list rather than merging it, and the save is refused when the map changed
on the NAS meanwhile). Change image keeps the markers; Delete map asks first.
An account allowed to view maps but not edit them is told so at the first
edit.
.TP
.B Events
Browse real events with their type and time, filtered by event type, camera,
and time range. On Surveillance Station 9.3 and later, events and their types
come from the Event Center (motion, audio, tampering, people/vehicle/animal
object detection, intrusion, and more). Before 9.3 they are decoded from each
camera's own detected categories (brand-dependent), and advanced search can
match Any or All of several selected event types at once.
.TP
.B Notifications
Surveillance Station's notifications appear in the header bar's notification
popover with an unread badge.
.TP
.B Home Mode
Toggle Surveillance Station home mode from the header bar.
.TP
.B Two-Factor Authentication
MFA/OTP login support. Prompts for a 6-digit authenticator code when 2FA is
enabled and optionally registers as a trusted device to skip OTP on future
logins.
.TP
.B License Management
View, add, and delete camera licenses. Supports online and offline activation.
.TP
.B Settings
Choose which Surveillance Station stream profile Live View plays (see
.B camera_live_view_stream_profiles
under CONFIGURATION), tune the demuxer cache sizes used for each
streaming profile (plain RTSP, WebSocket muxed-audio, and silent WebSocket), the extra buffer added
automatically at high History playback speeds, the shared demuxer byte
cap, the cap on each player's back buffer, and the
speed-times-active-slots budget the Live View timeline's
History speed dropdown enforces, plus an on-screen readout of cache
depth/target/effective speed for diagnosing buffering, the POS receipt
overlay (on or off, text size, maximum lines, time on screen), and a
switch that decodes events as before Surveillance Station 9.3, for
testing. Each setting has its own reset-to-default button, plus one that
resets everything on the page at once. Changes apply to the next stream
that starts (the on-screen readout takes effect immediately, the receipt
overlay with the next receipt, the event decoding switch from the next
login, and the speed budget the next time the speed dropdown opens) and
persist across restarts.
.TP
.B About
Shows the version, license, and repository links, and checks the GitHub
releases page once per login for a newer version, marking the About entry
when one is available.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Enable debug logging to stderr.
Passwords, session tokens, usernames, one-time codes, stream keys, and
credentials embedded in stream URLs are redacted from the output, including inside
exception tracebacks.
A debug run also records one line a minute giving the app's own memory
use, open descriptor count and thread count, so a long unattended
session leaves a trend behind rather than only its final state.
.TP
.BR \-\-log\-file [\fB=\fPPATH]
Also write logs to a file, at the same redacted content and level
(WARNING, or DEBUG if \-\-debug is also given) as stderr, plus the two
things that reach stderr without going through the log at all: the
traceback of an uncaught exception, on the main thread or a worker one,
and the messages GTK and GLib print themselves. With
.I PATH
given, as
.BI \-\-log\-file= PATH
or
.BI "\-\-log\-file " PATH\fR,
writes there, truncated on every run. Without it, writes to an
automatically named file under
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/surveillance-station/logs/ ,
or
.I ~/.local/state/surveillance-station/logs/
if
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME
is not set, and marks it complete on a clean exit (window close,
Ctrl-C, SIGTERM, or SIGHUP when not started under nohup) by creating a
same-named
.I .complete
sentinel file alongside it \(em an abnormal exit (a crash, or the
process being killed) leaves the log without one. The next run that
also passes a bare
.B \-\-log\-file
deletes every previous log marked complete this way along with its
sentinel; one left over from an abnormal exit is kept for inspection,
so disk usage only grows across repeated crashes, not from ordinary
use. The automatically named file is created mode 0600.
.PP
The options below control the Live View and the page shown. When the
application is already running, a second launch hands them over to the
running instance and exits, with status 2 if a value is invalid; a first
launch carries them out once logged in and the camera list has loaded.
The Live View options switch to Live View first. Combined, they apply in
the order grid, camera, seek or live, then page. Anything that cannot
apply, such as a camera ID the NAS does not have, is noted in the log and
skipped.
.TP
.BI \-\-grid " LAYOUT"
Switch Live View to the
.BR 1x1 ,
.BR 2x2 ,
.B 3x3
or
.B 4x4
grid, as the grid menu in the header bar does.
.TP
.BI \-\-camera " ID"
Show the camera with Surveillance Station ID
.I ID
in the slot the timeline tracks.
.TP
.B \-\-solo
With
.BR \-\-camera ,
show that camera alone, in 1x1, instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-seek " TIME"
Play every Live View slot from
.IR TIME ,
an ISO 8601 date and time such as
.BR 2026-01-31T22:15 ,
local time unless it carries an offset or
.BR Z ,
as the timeline's calendar Jump does.
.TP
.B \-\-live
Return every Live View slot to the live stream, as the timeline's Live
button does.
.TP
.BI \-\-page " PAGE"
Switch to
.BR live ,
.BR recordings ,
.BR events ,
.BR snapshots ,
.BR timelapse ,
.B maps
or
.BR settings .
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is stored in TOML format at:
.PP
.I ~/.config/surveillance-station/config.toml
.PP
Connection profiles define NAS host, port, HTTPS, and SSL verification settings.
Credentials are stored securely via the system keyring. When two-factor
authentication is enabled, trusted device tokens are stored per profile in the
config file.
When a session expires the client logs in again with the password it
connected with. If the NAS refuses that login, for instance because the
password was changed elsewhere, a two-factor code is needed, or the account
is disabled, blocked or locked, the client stops trying until the next login.
.PP
The
.B [general]
section stores the theme, the polling intervals, the snapshot directory,
.IR sidebar_visible ,
which records whether the camera sidebar is shown,
.IR timeline_visible ,
which records whether the Live View timeline strip is shown, and
.IR dismissed_update_version ,
the release tag whose update notice has been dismissed.
.PP
.IR poll_interval_cameras ", " poll_interval_alerts ", " poll_interval_homemode
and
.I poll_interval_transactions
are in seconds and default to 30, 30, 60 and 5. The minimum is 5: a smaller or
non-numeric value is reported in the log and replaced, since 0 would poll the
NAS continuously.
.IR poll_interval_cameras
also paces recovery: a live view slot whose stream gave up is retried on the
next camera poll, so it is the interval between one attempt and the next.
.PP
The
.B [session]
section stores the grid layout, active page and recording search filters
(including time presets). It is updated automatically so the application
restores the previous viewing session on restart. Critical changes are
flushed to disk immediately for crash resilience.
.PP
Settings keyed by camera ID are kept per profile, under its own
.B [profiles.\fIname\fB]
table, since every NAS numbers its cameras from 1: the camera assignments
per layout
.RI ( layout_cameras ),
the cameras picked for searches
.RI ( search_camera_ids ", " events_search_camera_ids ", " snapshots_search_camera_ids ),
the E-Map last opened on the Maps page
.RI ( last_emap_id ),
and the sections below. A config from an earlier version kept one shared
set at the top level and in
.BR [session] ;
it is read as the default profile's.
.PP
The
.B camera_protocols
section selects the stream protocol per camera (auto, websocket,
mjpeg, rtsp_over_http, rtsp, multicast, direct).
.B auto
is the same as
.BR websocket ;
there is no fallback between protocols, so pick one explicitly if
WebSocket does not work for a camera.
.IR websocket
uses a WebSocket stream bridged to mpv via an in-memory pipe, muxing in
real audio via ffmpeg when the camera's audio codec is PCMU or AAC.
When set to
.IR direct ,
the URL from
.B camera_overrides
is used instead of Synology's RTSP proxy.
.PP
The
.B camera_live_view_stream_profiles
section selects the Surveillance Station stream profile per camera
(high, balanced, low) for a WebSocket stream; a camera with no entry
follows its Live View setting in Surveillance Station. The Settings
page's
.B Live View stream profile
overrides every camera's entry unless it is set to
.BR "Use camera settings" ,
its default. An RTSP stream always carries the camera's Live View
setting in Surveillance Station, and a direct URL decides its stream
itself.
.PP
The
.B camera_dewarp
section selects a fisheye dewarp per camera,
.B panorama
or
.BR ptz ,
and
.B camera_fisheye_mount
how it hangs,
.B wall
for a lens looking straight out; a camera with no entry is shown as
delivered, and one with no mount is on a ceiling, looking down.
.PP
These settings can be configured from
.B Camera Settings
by right-clicking a camera in the sidebar.
.PP
The
.B camera_fisheye_lens
section, set from Camera Settings with
.B Calibrate\[u2026]
and
.BR Apply ,
or by hand, calibrates a fisheye lens per camera when
the default, a centred full circle of 180 degrees, does not fit:
.RI \(dq center_x , center_y , radius , fov \(dq,
the centre as fractions of the frame width and height, the radius as a
fraction of its height and the lens's field of view in degrees.
A missing or invalid field keeps its default.
.PP
The
.B camera_volume
and
.B camera_muted
sections hold the Live View volume (0-100) and mute state per camera, as set
from the slot's hover toolbar. Cameras start muted.
.PP
The
.B legacy_event_type_history
section (also read under its old name,
.BR event_type_history )
caches which event types each camera has ever produced, as
discovered by the Live View timeline's
.B Filter events
popover. Each camera's entry holds
.IR types ,
a list of (event type flag, reserved) pairs, and
.IR checked_until ,
the unix time its recording history has been scanned up to, so a later
open only scans forward from there rather than repeating the whole
history. Written automatically; deleting a camera's entry forces a full
rescan the next time the popover is opened. Not used on Surveillance
Station 9.3 and later, where the popover lists every event type the NAS
supports without scanning.
.PP
The
.BR [setting_overrides] ,
.B [setting_overrides_bool]
and
.B [setting_overrides_choice]
sections hold every value changed from its default on the
.B Settings
page (the Live View stream profile, player cache sizes, the demuxer byte
and back buffer caps, the on-screen cache/speed
readout, the History speed dropdown's speed-times-slots budget, the POS
receipt overlay, and the switch that decodes events as before Surveillance
Station 9.3 from the next login, for testing), split
by value type since TOML distinguishes numbers, booleans and text. Written by
the Settings page itself; use its own reset buttons rather than
hand-editing these.
.PP
The
.B [webhooks]
section, set by hand only, makes the client POST what it sees as JSON to
each URL in
.IR targets :
the events listed in
.IR events ,
out of
.BR alert ,
.BR camera_offline ,
.BR camera_online ,
.BR home_mode ,
.B snapshot_saved
and
.B layout_changed
(all six by default), each POST taking at most
.I timeout_seconds
(3 by default, 30 at most). With no targets nothing is sent. The section
is read at startup and written back as it was then on every save, so edit
it with the application closed. A target is sent an
.B Authorization: Bearer
header when the keyring holds a token for it, under the service
.I surveillance-station
as
.I webhook_token:
followed by the target URL as written in
.IR targets .
.SH KEYBOARD SHORTCUTS
.TP
.B Ctrl+Q
Quit the application.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.TP
.B System (Linux)
gtk4, libmpv, portaudio, ffmpeg, gir1.2-gtk-4.0
.TP
.B System (FreeBSD)
gtk4, mpv, portaudio, ffmpeg, py311-gobject3, py311-cairo
.TP
.B System (OpenBSD)
gtk4, mpv, portaudio, ffmpeg, py3-gobject3, py3-cairo
.TP
.B Python
PyGObject (>=3.50), httpx, python-mpv, PyOpenGL, keyring, tomli-w, websockets,
sounddevice, cryptography
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/surveillance-station/config.toml
Application configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/surveillance-station/snapshots/
Default snapshot download directory.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/surveillance-station/viewer/
The image a snapshot viewer is showing
.RI ( $XDG_CACHE_HOME
when set). A viewer still open when the application exits leaves its
file behind, and the next run's first viewer removes it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SURVEILLANCE_AO
Audio output driver handed to mpv, taking any name
.B mpv \-\-ao
accepts, for example
.IR pulse ,
.I pipewire
or
.IR alsa .
Left unset, mpv chooses for itself. The AppImage sets it to
.I pulse
where it has to run its own bundled PipeWire library, whose output driver
would crash there; setting it explicitly overrides that.
.TP
.B SURVEILLANCE_MPV_OPTS
Further options for the embedded mpv player, separated by whitespace and
written as
.I name=value
the way
.I mpv.conf
spells them, or a bare
.I name
for a flag, as on mpv's command line. Quote a value containing whitespace
as in a shell. They are applied after the client's own options and
replace them by name, the driver chosen through
.B SURVEILLANCE_AO
included. A name mpv does not know stops the player from starting, and
the log names it. For example
.B hwdec=nvdec hwdec-extra-frames=12
picks NVIDIA decoding and enlarges its surface pool; see
.I TROUBLESHOOTING.md
for when that is needed.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
See
.I TROUBLESHOOTING.md
in the source repository for common problems and fixes
(HTTP 502, playback failures, download errors, AppImage audio crashes,
Ubuntu 24.04 / AppImage notes).
.SH AUTHORS
Surveillance Station Client contributors.
